{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794852834292,"sessionID":"ses_e1a82d916ffe3W1SLL4OkhFuu6","part":{"partID":"prt_1e5906b00001ppjtHD2q8GchoM","sessionID":"ses_e1a82d916ffe3W1SLL4OkhFuu6","messageID":"msg_1e59038c8001m0vKcO9uS3VmR9","type":"tool","id":"call_62c957794b604204a204ba5b","tool":"write","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"content":"# Review Iteration 002\n\n## Dispatcher\n\n- Mode: `review`; target `specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build`; lineage `routing-11`; iteration 2 of 8; status `complete`.\n- Review focus: brief 11's routing area; this iteration covers the security relevance of the retained vocabulary and the maintainability of the propagation contract behind the router edit. Holdout rule observed: no prompt corpus opened.\n- Dimensions this iteration: `security`, `maintainability`.\n\n## Files Reviewed\n\n- `.skilled/skills/sk-doc/ROUTER.md:126-142` and `:218-220` (narrowed prose and keyword lists)\n- `.skilled/skills/sk-doc/graph-metadata.json:340-395` (committed signal bag)\n- `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/skill-graph.json:1` (all 14 hub signal bags)\n- `.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-skill/scripts/generate-router-intent-signals.cjs:12-122`\n- `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/scorer/text.ts:7-35` (tokenize and stop words)\n- `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/scorer/lanes/lexical.ts:60-105`\n- `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/scorer/lane-registry.ts:6-18`\n- `specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/039-main-sk-doc-routing-repair/scratch/readme-probes.txt:1-11`\n- `specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/039-main-sk-doc-routing-repair/spec.md:60-111`\n\n## Findings - New\n\n### P0 Findings\n\nNone.\n\n### P1 Findings\n\nNone.\n\n### P2 Findings\n\n4. **The narrowing removes the git and gate words but leaves the same cross-hub token class from the other named hooks** -- `.skilled/skills/sk-doc/ROUTER.md:219` -- The prose at `ROUTER.md:136-137` says the `git-*` and `spec-gate` hooks are not named because their names carry \"git\", \"hooks\" and \"gate\", which belong to git and spec work. After the removal the committed sk-doc signal bag has no `git` and no `gate` token, but `hooks` remains because `codex-global-hooks-check hook readme` is still named (`graph-metadata.json:358-359`), and the other retained names still inject tokens that belong to other hubs: `env` and `secret` (env-secret-guard, `graph-metadata.json:368`), `session` and `handover` (session-handover, `:382`), `decision` and `capture` (decision-capture, `:362`), `dispatch` and `subagent` (subagent-dispatch-guard), `advisor` and `freshness` (skill-advisor, dist-freshness), `overlay`, `completion` and `quality`. The current probe is still correct (`readme-probes.txt:8` shows `git hooks => sk-git`), so this is an incomplete principle and a latent ambiguity, not a present misroute.\n   Finding class: class-of-bug\n   Scope proof: Tokenized all 345 committed sk-doc signals with the scorer's `tokenize` rule (`text.ts:31-35`) and intersected the 39 tokens carried by the 44 hook phrases with the 13 other hubs' signal bags. Collisions exist for cli-classifier, cli-external-orchestration, sk-code, sk-design, sk-git, sk-prompt, system-skill-advisor, system-skilled-doctor and system-spec-kit.\n   Affected surface hints: `.skilled/skills/sk-doc/ROUTER.md` `HOOK_README` list, `.skilled/skills/sk-doc/graph-metadata.json`, `system-spec-kit` and `cli-external-orchestration` vocabularies\n   Case: \"check the env secret guard hook\" shares `env`, `secret`, `guard`, `check` and `hook` with sk-doc, and \"check the git hooks\" shares `hooks` and `check`; neither is a README request. The lexical lane's weight is 0.28 (`lane-registry.ts:10`), so shared tokens alone do not prove the advisor's 0.8 surfacing threshold is crossed; the finding is the unapplied principle, not a proven route failure.\n\n5. **The generator's carry rule and its `--check` gate share a blind spot for dotted phrases** -- `.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-skill/scripts/generate-router-intent-signals.cjs:57` -- `declaredPhrases` keeps only phrases that contain a space, so `security.md` and `sync.md` are never candidates for `intent_signals`. `--check` therefore reports `missing=0` even though `SECURITY_POLICY` declares `security.md` as its only keyword (`ROUTER.md:218`) and neither generated artifact contains it. The packet probe shows a `SECURITY.md` request still tops sk-doc (`readme-probes.txt:7`), so reach is not broken; the defect is that the gate cannot verify the contract this change relies on.\n   Finding class: matrix/evidence\n   Scope proof: Computed the declared vocabularies with and without the `k.includes(' ')` filter: 381 total phrases, 328 multi-word, and `security.md` plus `sync.md` appear only in the excluded set. Ran `--check --hub sk-doc` and read `missing=0`.\n   Affected surface hints: `.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-skill/scripts/generate-router-intent-signals.cjs`, `.skilled/skills/sk-doc/ROUTER.md` `SECURITY_POLICY` and `RUNTIME_FOLDER`, `.skilled/skills/sk-doc/graph-metadata.json`\n   Case: a future router edit relies on `security.md` or `sync.md` as its only phrase; `--check` passes and the advisor vocabulary silently lacks the intent, so only a live probe catches it.\n\n## Traceability Checks\n\n- `spec_code` -- status `pass`. The narrowed vocabulary matches the phase's scope; the generator blind spot in finding 5 is a contract gap in a named integration surface, not a divergence in the changed files.\n- `checklist_evidence` -- status `notApplicable`, `applicable: false`. Same reason as iteration 1: this review has no packet checklist of its own.\n- `skill_agent` -- status `pass`. No agent definition is touched, and the probe file still routes the three README families to sk-doc.\n- `agent_cross_runtime` -- status `notApplicable`, `applicable: false`.\n- `feature_catalog_code` -- status `partial`. The two-stage contract holds for every multi-word phrase, but finding 5 names the dotted-phrase case the gate cannot see.\n- `playbook_capability` -- status `pass`. The committed probes cover the security-adjacent prompts and the bare git, rules, standards and runtime-mirror prompts; no playbook scenario was executed, so the status rests on the packet's recorded probes plus the local reproduction.\n\n## Integration Evidence\n\n- Token intersection from the committed graph: 44 hook phrases carry 39 tokens; the tokens that also appear in another hub's bag include `env`, `secret` (system-spec-kit), `session`, `handover` (system-spec-kit), `dispatch`, `subagent` (cli-external-orchestration), `advisor`, `freshness` (system-skill-advisor), `decision`, `capture` (system-spec-kit, sk-code, sk-design), `completion`, `quality` (sk-code) and `hooks` (system-skill-advisor, sk-git).\n- `generate-router-intent-signals.cjs --check --hub sk-doc` -> `declared=328 signals=345 missing=0 orphans=0`, `RESULT: PASSED`. The 328 count is exactly the phrases that contain a space; `security.md` and `sync.md` are outside it.\n- The packet probe records `create a SECURITY.md for this repo => sk-doc, system-spec-kit` and `git hooks => sk-git`, so both the security reach and the bare-git exclusion are currently correct.\n- Lexical lane weight `0.28` from `lane-registry.ts:10`; token overlap is one lane, not the whole confidence, so no finding here claims a route failure from token overlap alone.\n\n## Edge Cases\n\n- A retained named hook phrase can be the only reason a foreign token enters the sk-doc bag, yet the owning hub still wins on exact-phrase evidence. The finding is recorded as a latent ambiguity, not as a present wrong route, because no probe shows a misroute.\n- `hooks` is the one word the prose names that survives the removal. `codex-global-hooks-check` is a named shared hook and was not part of the phase's removal set, so the prose's reason is only two-thirds true; a reader cannot tell that from the sentence.\n- The generator's docstring says \"Single words are left behind deliberately\" (`generate-router-intent-signals.cjs:27-28`); a dotted filename is not a single word in the matching sense, so the filter's effect on `security.md` is broader than the stated rationale.\n- The `security policy` phrase removal is security-positive: a generic security-policy request no longer shares the `security` token with sk-doc. The probe line 7 confirms the retained `SECURITY.md` path still reaches the hub.\n\n## Confirmed-Clean Surfaces\n\n- No `git` or `gate` token remains in the committed sk-doc signal bag; the three words the prose names are reduced to `hooks` only.\n- Bare `git hooks`, `git rules`, `git standards` still reach sk-git (probe lines 8-10), and `runtime mirror drift` still reaches `command-doctor-runtime-mirrors` (line 11).\n- `SECURITY_POLICY` still reaches sk-doc for a `SECURITY.md` request (probe line 7) despite its phrase being absent from the generated signals.\n- The retained plugin, runtime-folder and named-hook phrases still select their specific leaves under the documented stage-two scorer.\n\n## Ruled Out\n\n- A present misroute from the residual tokens: no probe or scorer result shows sk-doc beating the owning hub for a bare env, session, decision or dispatch prompt; the lexical lane alone carries 0.28 (iteration 2).\n- A broken security reach after dropping `security policy`: probe line 7 tops sk-doc; ruled out in iteration 1 and rechecked here (iteration 2).\n- A carried dotted phrase in either generated artifact: `grep` over `graph-metadata.json` and `skill-graph.json` returns no `security.md` or `sync.md`; recorded as finding 5, not as parity failure (iteration 2).\n\n## Next Focus\n\n- Dimension: all four, cross-reference and verification.\n- Focus area: close the loop on the six traceability protocols, recheck the three P2 surfaces against the final registry, and decide whether the two maintainability advisories change the release verdict.\n- Reason: correctness and traceability are covered from iteration 1, security and maintainability from this iteration; the remaining work is protocol closure and consistency of the final artifacts.\n- Rotation status: cross-reference pass, no new dimension.\n- Productive carry-forward: exact signal-set parity, the packet probe, and the documented stage-two scorer.\n- Required evidence: registry counts, final state log records, and the same three integration checks.\n- Recovery note: none.\n\nReview verdict: PASS\n","path":"specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/review/lineages/routing-11/iterations/iteration-002.md"},"output":"Created file successfully: specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/review/lineages/routing-11/iterations/iteration-002.md","title":"write","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Created file successfully: specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/review/lineages/routing-11/iterations/iteration-002.md"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"content\":\"# Review Iteration 002\\n\\n## Dispatcher\\n\\n- Mode: `review`; target `specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build`; lineage `routing-11`; iteration 2 of 8; status `complete`.\\n- Review focus: brief 11's routing area; this iteration covers the security relevance of the retained vocabulary and the maintainability of the propagation contract behind the router edit. Holdout rule observed: no prompt corpus opened.\\n- Dimensions this iteration: `security`, `maintainability`.\\n\\n## Files Reviewed\\n\\n- `.skilled/skills/sk-doc/ROUTER.md:126-142` and `:218-220` (narrowed prose and keyword lists)\\n- `.skilled/skills/sk-doc/graph-metadata.json:340-395` (committed signal bag)\\n- `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/skill-graph.json:1` (all 14 hub signal bags)\\n- `.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-skill/scripts/generate-router-intent-signals.cjs:12-122`\\n- `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/scorer/text.ts:7-35` (tokenize and stop words)\\n- `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/scorer/lanes/lexical.ts:60-105`\\n- `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/scorer/lane-registry.ts:6-18`\\n- `specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/039-main-sk-doc-routing-repair/scratch/readme-probes.txt:1-11`\\n- `specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/039-main-sk-doc-routing-repair/spec.md:60-111`\\n\\n## Findings - New\\n\\n### P0 Findings\\n\\nNone.\\n\\n### P1 Findings\\n\\nNone.\\n\\n### P2 Findings\\n\\n4. **The narrowing removes the git and gate words but leaves the same cross-hub token class from the other named hooks** -- `.skilled/skills/sk-doc/ROUTER.md:219` -- The prose at `ROUTER.md:136-137` says the `git-*` and `spec-gate` hooks are not named because their names carry \\\"git\\\", \\\"hooks\\\" and \\\"gate\\\", which belong to git and spec work. After the removal the committed sk-doc signal bag has no `git` and no `gate` token, but `hooks` remains because `codex-global-hooks-check hook readme` is still named (`graph-metadata.json:358-359`), and the other retained names still inject tokens that belong to other hubs: `env` and `secret` (env-secret-guard, `graph-metadata.json:368`), `session` and `handover` (session-handover, `:382`), `decision` and `capture` (decision-capture, `:362`), `dispatch` and `subagent` (subagent-dispatch-guard), `advisor` and `freshness` (skill-advisor, dist-freshness), `overlay`, `completion` and `quality`. The current probe is still correct (`readme-probes.txt:8` shows `git hooks => sk-git`), so this is an incomplete principle and a latent ambiguity, not a present misroute.\\n   Finding class: class-of-bug\\n   Scope proof: Tokenized all 345 committed sk-doc signals with the scorer's `tokenize` rule (`text.ts:31-35`) and intersected the 39 tokens carried by the 44 hook phrases with the 13 other hubs' signal bags. Collisions exist for cli-classifier, cli-external-orchestration, sk-code, sk-design, sk-git, sk-prompt, system-skill-advisor, system-skilled-doctor and system-spec-kit.\\n   Affected surface hints: `.skilled/skills/sk-doc/ROUTER.md` `HOOK_README` list, `.skilled/skills/sk-doc/graph-metadata.json`, `system-spec-kit` and `cli-external-orchestration` vocabularies\\n   Case: \\\"check the env secret guard hook\\\" shares `env`, `secret`, `guard`, `check` and `hook` with sk-doc, and \\\"check the git hooks\\\" shares `hooks` and `check`; neither is a README request. The lexical lane's weight is 0.28 (`lane-registry.ts:10`), so shared tokens alone do not prove the advisor's 0.8 surfacing threshold is crossed; the finding is the unapplied principle, not a proven route failure.\\n\\n5. **The generator's carry rule and its `--check` gate share a blind spot for dotted phrases** -- `.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-skill/scripts/generate-router-intent-signals.cjs:57` -- `declaredPhrases` keeps only phrases that contain a space, so `security.md` and `sync.md` are never candidates for `intent_signals`. `--check` therefore reports `missing=0` even though `SECURITY_POLICY` declares `security.md` as its only keyword (`ROUTER.md:218`) and neither generated artifact contains it. The packet probe shows a `SECURITY.md` request still tops sk-doc (`readme-probes.txt:7`), so reach is not broken; the defect is that the gate cannot verify the contract this change relies on.\\n   Finding class: matrix/evidence\\n   Scope proof: Computed the declared vocabularies with and without the `k.includes(' ')` filter: 381 total phrases, 328 multi-word, and `security.md` plus `sync.md` appear only in the excluded set. Ran `--check --hub sk-doc` and read `missing=0`.\\n   Affected surface hints: `.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-skill/scripts/generate-router-intent-signals.cjs`, `.skilled/skills/sk-doc/ROUTER.md` `SECURITY_POLICY` and `RUNTIME_FOLDER`, `.skilled/skills/sk-doc/graph-metadata.json`\\n   Case: a future router edit relies on `security.md` or `sync.md` as its only phrase; `--check` passes and the advisor vocabulary silently lacks the intent, so only a live probe catches it.\\n\\n## Traceability Checks\\n\\n- `spec_code` -- status `pass`. The narrowed vocabulary matches the phase's scope; the generator blind spot in finding 5 is a contract gap in a named integration surface, not a divergence in the changed files.\\n- `checklist_evidence` -- status `notApplicable`, `applicable: false`. Same reason as iteration 1: this review has no packet checklist of its own.\\n- `skill_agent` -- status `pass`. No agent definition is touched, and the probe file still routes the three README families to sk-doc.\\n- `agent_cross_runtime` -- status `notApplicable`, `applicable: false`.\\n- `feature_catalog_code` -- status `partial`. The two-stage contract holds for every multi-word phrase, but finding 5 names the dotted-phrase case the gate cannot see.\\n- `playbook_capability` -- status `pass`. The committed probes cover the security-adjacent prompts and the bare git, rules, standards and runtime-mirror prompts; no playbook scenario was executed, so the status rests on the packet's recorded probes plus the local reproduction.\\n\\n## Integration Evidence\\n\\n- Token intersection from the committed graph: 44 hook phrases carry 39 tokens; the tokens that also appear in another hub's bag include `env`, `secret` (system-spec-kit), `session`, `handover` (system-spec-kit), `dispatch`, `subagent` (cli-external-orchestration), `advisor`, `freshness` (system-skill-advisor), `decision`, `capture` (system-spec-kit, sk-code, sk-design), `completion`, `quality` (sk-code) and `hooks` (system-skill-advisor, sk-git).\\n- `generate-router-intent-signals.cjs --check --hub sk-doc` -> `declared=328 signals=345 missing=0 orphans=0`, `RESULT: PASSED`. The 328 count is exactly the phrases that contain a space; `security.md` and `sync.md` are outside it.\\n- The packet probe records `create a SECURITY.md for this repo => sk-doc, system-spec-kit` and `git hooks => sk-git`, so both the security reach and the bare-git exclusion are currently correct.\\n- Lexical lane weight `0.28` from `lane-registry.ts:10`; token overlap is one lane, not the whole confidence, so no finding here claims a route failure from token overlap alone.\\n\\n## Edge Cases\\n\\n- A retained named hook phrase can be the only reason a foreign token enters the sk-doc bag, yet the owning hub still wins on exact-phrase evidence. The finding is recorded as a latent ambiguity, not as a present wrong route, because no probe shows a misroute.\\n- `hooks` is the one word the prose names that survives the removal. `codex-global-hooks-check` is a named shared hook and was not part of the phase's removal set, so the prose's reason is only two-thirds true; a reader cannot tell that from the sentence.\\n- The generator's docstring says \\\"Single words are left behind deliberately\\\" (`generate-router-intent-signals.cjs:27-28`); a dotted filename is not a single word in the matching sense, so the filter's effect on `security.md` is broader than the stated rationale.\\n- The `security policy` phrase removal is security-positive: a generic security-policy request no longer shares the `security` token with sk-doc. The probe line 7 confirms the retained `SECURITY.md` path still reaches the hub.\\n\\n## Confirmed-Clean Surfaces\\n\\n- No `git` or `gate` token remains in the committed sk-doc signal bag; the three words the prose names are reduced to `hooks` only.\\n- Bare `git hooks`, `git rules`, `git standards` still reach sk-git (probe lines 8-10), and `runtime mirror drift` still reaches `command-doctor-runtime-mirrors` (line 11).\\n- `SECURITY_POLICY` still reaches sk-doc for a `SECURITY.md` request (probe line 7) despite its phrase being absent from the generated signals.\\n- The retained plugin, runtime-folder and named-hook phrases still select their specific leaves under the documented stage-two scorer.\\n\\n## Ruled Out\\n\\n- A present misroute from the residual tokens: no probe or scorer result shows sk-doc beating the owning hub for a bare env, session, decision or dispatch prompt; the lexical lane alone carries 0.28 (iteration 2).\\n- A broken security reach after dropping `security policy`: probe line 7 tops sk-doc; ruled out in iteration 1 and rechecked here (iteration 2).\\n- A carried dotted phrase in either generated artifact: `grep` over `graph-metadata.json` and `skill-graph.json` returns no `security.md` or `sync.md`; recorded as finding 5, not as parity failure (iteration 2).\\n\\n## Next Focus\\n\\n- Dimension: all four, cross-reference and verification.\\n- Focus area: close the loop on the six traceability protocols, recheck the three P2 surfaces against the final registry, and decide whether the two maintainability advisories change the release verdict.\\n- Reason: correctness and traceability are covered from iteration 1, security and maintainability from this iteration; the remaining work is protocol closure and consistency of the final artifacts.\\n- Rotation status: cross-reference pass, no new dimension.\\n- Productive carry-forward: exact signal-set parity, the packet probe, and the documented stage-two scorer.\\n- Required evidence: registry counts, final state log records, and the same three integration checks.\\n- Recovery note: none.\\n\\nReview verdict: PASS\\n\",\"path\":\"specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/review/lineages/routing-11/iterations/iteration-002.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1794852809472,"end":1794852834292}}}}
